'''KITSAT-3''' was a South Korean [[remote sensing]] [[Small satellite|minisatellite]] which carried MEIS (Multispectral Earth Imaging System) and SENSE (Space ENvironment Scientific Experiment) instruments to [[low Earth orbit]] (LEO). Launched on 26 May 1999 by Indian space agency [[Indian Space Research Organisation]] (ISRO), on [[orbit]] the satellite was renamed to '''Uribyol-3'''. Manufactured by [[KAIST Satellite Technology Research Center]] (SaTReC), KITSAT-3 was developed with experience from [[KITSAT-1]] and [[KITSAT-2]] (no heritage to the KITSAT-1 and KITSAT-2 bus) and was the first independently designed South Korean [[satellite]].<ref name="KITSAT series – KITSAT-3">{{cite news|url=http://satrec.kaist.ac.kr/e_02_01_02.php|title=KITSAT series – KITSAT-3|publisher=KAIST Satellite Technology Research Center |access-date=9 July 2016}}</ref><ref name="KITSAT-3">{{cite news|title=KITSAT-3|publisher=eoportal.org|access-date=9 July 2016|url=https://directory.eoportal.org/web/eoportal/satellite-missions/k/kitsat-3#foot15%29}}</ref><ref name="Kitsat 3 (Uribyol 3)">{{cite news|title=Kitsat 3 (Uribyol 3)|publisher=Gunter's Space Page|access-date=9 July 2016|url=http://space.skyrocket.de/doc_sdat/kitsat-3.htm}}</ref><ref name="Kitsat 3">{{cite news|url=https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/nmc/spacecraft/display.action?id=1999-029B|title=KITSAT 3|publisher=NASA|access-date=9 July 2016}} {{PD-notice}}</ref>

== Launch ==
Kitsat-3 was launched in the [[PSLV-C2]] mission by 26 May 1999 by Indian space agency [[Indian Space Research Organisation|ISRO]] at 06:22 [[Coordinated Universal Time|UTC]] from [[Satish Dhawan Space Centre]] in India. The launch was the first commercial launch by ISRO of its [[launch vehicle]] ([[PSLV-C2]]) and US$1.0 million (equivalent to US${{Inflation|US|1|1999|r=2}} million in {{Inflation-year|US}}) was charged by the Indian agency for launching and injecting the satellite in the low Earth orbit.<ref name="The science and commerce of PSLV">{{cite news |url=http://www.frontline.in/static/html/fl1612/16120370.htm|title=The science and commerce of PSLV|publisher=Frontline (magazine)|access-date=9 July 2016}}</ref>

== Mission highlights ==
* First independently designed [[South Korea]]n [[satellite]]
* First commercial flight of the launch vehicle, [[Polar Satellite Launch Vehicle]] (PSLV)
* Spacecraft attitude was first captured and 3-axis stabilized on 30 May 1999
* First image obtained on 31 May 1999
* [[Solar panels on spacecraft|Solar panels]] deployed on 24 June 1999
* Design for life of 3 years
* However, operations ended in December 2003 (4.5 years achieved)<ref name="KITSAT series – KITSAT-3"/><ref name="KITSAT-3"/><ref name="Kitsat 3 (Uribyol 3)"/><ref name="Kitsat 3"/>

